diff --git a/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md b/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md
index 874d070b1..d2d4fafd0 100644
--- a/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md
+++ b/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md
@@ -169,6 +169,8 @@ A single, evidence-based map of how far bex actually matches [render.com](https:
 | Key Value (Valkey / Redis) | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | REST `keyvalue/rest.go` `/v1/key-value` (list/get/create/delete/`connection-info` + `suspend`/`resume`); **Delete teardown (w7/m12):** the Valkey StatefulSet carries `PersistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy{WhenDeleted: Delete}` (k8s 1.23+ feature, GA in 1.30) so PVCs are automatically garbage-collected when the StatefulSet is deleted, rather than being orphaned as they are by default. The StatefulSet and credentials Secret are cascaded via ownerRefs on the KeyValue CR. GraphQL `keyValue*` (`keyValues`/`keyValue`/`keyValueConnectionInfo`/`keyValueInstanceTypes`, `createKeyValue`/`deleteKeyValue`/`suspendKeyValue`/`resumeKeyValue`); MCP retains Render's `list_key_value`/`get_key_value`/`create_key_value` set and adds `suspend_keyvalue`/`resume_keyvalue` as documented bex lifecycle/safety extensions; no datastore delete tool is added. **`status: suspended` (w5/061):** hibernated stores report `status: "suspended"` (same Render `databaseStatus` vocabulary Key Value reuses) alongside `suspended: "suspended"` so `bex keyvalues get` — whose `KeyValueOut` has no Suspended field — prints `Status: suspended` instead of a false `available`. Mechanism (w1/m14): a `KeyValue` CR → single-instance Valkey + internal Service DNS + credentials Secret, optional public end-to-end TLS through the shared metered Key Value SNI proxy; suspend scales the StatefulSet to zero (PVC/Secret kept, data + password survive). Plans `free`/`starter`/`standard` (the web-service vocabulary, **not** Postgres `basic-*`); connection-info 3 keys — `internalConnectionString` (`redis://`), `externalConnectionString` (`rediss://` TLS, when public), `cliCommand` (private `redis-cli -u …`; public `redis-cli --sni <externalHost> -u …`). **Access control (w7/m5):** `ipAllowList` is enforced with the environment layer by that proxy before backend connection: settable at create on all three adapters (`ipAllowList` on the REST body / `createKeyValue` arg / `create_key_value` arg), read back on every get/list, and editable via REST `GET/PUT /v1/key-value/{id}/ip-allow-list`, the REST `PATCH /v1/key-value/{id}` `ipAllowList` field (**w7/m45** — the official CLI's `keyvalues update --ip-allow-list`/`--clear-ip-allow-list` path; a non-nil empty list clears it), GraphQL `keyValueIpAllowList`/`setKeyValueIpAllowList`, and MCP `update_key_value(ipAllowList:)` (**w7/m45**, closing the earlier MCP allowlist-tool gap; folded from `set_key_value_ip_allow_list` in w1/m71). REST uses Render's structured `[{cidrBlock, description}]` entries; the separately named GraphQL/MCP string-list arguments remain supported and are lifted explicitly. **Stable `red-` id + rename (w9/m6):** a store now carries an immutable minted `red-<xid>` id (`metadata.name`) and a separate mutable display name (`spec.name`), the same split Postgres shipped in w9/m3 — so `keyvalues update <name> --name <new>` routes by the opaque id and survives rename. `PATCH /v1/key-value/{id}` accepts a `name` field (Render's all-pointer partial-update semantics; `dryRun` supported); GraphQL adds `renameKeyValue`, MCP adds `update_key_value(name:)` (a bex extension — Render's MCP server has no KV rename tool, same as the Postgres rename; both folded into their patch tools at w1/m74). The w1/m56 fleet gate verified zero missing names/non-typed ids before making `spec.name` required and retiring the fallback plus one-time script. This closes the last name-as-id datastore deviation ([ADR020-identifiers.md](ADR020-identifiers.md) § Known deviations, [ADR021-keyvalue-management.md](ADR021-keyvalue-management.md) §6). **Divergences (conscious):** the internal URL always mints a password (Render leaves it unauthenticated by default — bex is stricter, a safe superset). **Maxmemory Policy / Persistence Mode (w5/011):** now backed by the `KeyValue` CR (`spec.maxmemoryPolicy`/`spec.persistenceMode`) — the operator maps them onto Valkey's `--maxmemory-policy` (plus an `--maxmemory` budget at 80% of the plan RAM) and its AOF/RDB persistence args; settable at create on all three adapters (REST body / `createKeyValue` arg / `create_key_value` arg) and read back on get/list, no longer omitted. **Updatable post-create (w7/m45):** `maxmemoryPolicy` now mutates after create via REST `PATCH /v1/key-value/{id}` (the official CLI's `keyvalues update --memory-policy`), GraphQL `setKeyValueMaxmemoryPolicy`, and MCP `update_key_value(maxmemoryPolicy:)` — all routing through the shared `UpdateKeyValue`, which normalizes the underscore/hyphen forms identically to create; dashboard eviction-policy editing after create is a tracked follow-up. **`persistenceMode` too (w6/m127):** it joins the same PATCH set — REST `PATCH /v1/key-value/{id}` `persistenceMode` (Render's fifth `keyValuePATCHInput` property, closing a create-only divergence), GraphQL `setKeyValuePersistenceMode`, MCP `update_key_value(persistenceMode:)` — through the same `UpdateKeyValue`/`validate`, so a store created on `off` can reach durable `journal_snapshot` without being recreated (the operator's `valkeyArgs` re-derives the AOF/RDB flags and rolls the pod, exactly as a `maxmemoryPolicy` change does). Dashboard persistence editing after create is the same tracked follow-up as eviction policy. **Dashboard (UI ✅, w5/m12):** `/keyvalue` sidebar entry + list (stat cards, status chips, empty state), `/keyvalue/new` full-page create form (name, tier cards from `keyValueInstanceTypes`, Valkey version, public toggle — a single page, matching Render's own `/new/redis` layout captured live rather than m8's dialog pattern, [docs/render-artifacts/key-value.md](render-artifacts/key-value.md)), `/keyvalue/$id` detail (status/plan metadata, on-demand connection-info reveal with internal/external/CLI fields + copy, suspend-with-confirm/resume, typed-name delete confirm). A Networking section on the detail page (w7/m5) reads/edits the IP allowlist (Render's "Networking" section), with the internal-only state called out. **Plan update (m16):** `PATCH /v1/key-value/{id}` (`plan` field) / GraphQL `updateKeyValuePlan` / MCP `update_key_value(plan:)` / dashboard detail-page plan-picker card — patches `KeyValue.spec.plan`; the operator re-reconciles the Valkey StatefulSet resources on the next sync (rolling update, data preserved). Valid plans: `free`/`starter`/`standard` (validated against `tiers.Valkey`; unknown plan returns 400). **w5/011 + w6/m127:** the create form carries the Maxmemory Policy / Persistence Mode dropdowns; **both are selectable on every plan, the Free tier included.** The form once copied Render's "Free plan has no persistent disk, so persistence is forced Off" lock, but that premise is false for bex — `tiers.yaml` gives the free Valkey tier a 1 GB disk (a **conscious divergence**, more generous than Render's 25 MB / no-persistence free tier), so a form-created free store now defaults to durable `journal_snapshot` and matches the API create path instead of being a silent in-memory cache. The detail page's `DatastoreMetricsPanel` (w3/m10) now shows Memory + Connections charts for a Key Value (redis_exporter sidecar → Prometheus `valkey-instances` scrape → the shared `datastoreMetrics` verb's `kv_memory`/`kv_connections`), alongside its disk-usage chart. **REST list filter parity (w2/m53):** `GET /v1/key-value` now supports `?environmentId=` (OR'd) and the four RFC3339 time-window params; `?suspended=suspended`\|`not_suspended` is a **bex extension** (Render's OpenAPI lists it only on `/postgres` — admitted via `renderQueryExtensions`, named 400 on unknown value; w5/060); `?name=` now also matches by the opaque `red-` id (not only by display name). **Readiness truth (w1/m166, 2026-10-02):** `available` is published from an authenticated-PING readiness probe (a `-LOADING`/`NOAUTH` reply fails it), and the internal `<id>` Service is ClusterIP rather than headless, so neither in-cluster clients nor the public `kv-sni-proxy` resolve a CoreDNS-cached stale pod IP or NXDOMAIN after a restart (production measured a ~28s post-`available` flap from exactly that). Status vocabulary is unchanged (`creating` / `config_restart` / `available` / `suspended`); REST, GraphQL, MCP and dashboard all read the one `kvStatus` mapping. |
 | Persistent disks | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Render `/disks` + Disks tab. Was a deliberate non-goal (stateless-first); **reversed 2026-08-22 by [ADR082](ADR082-persistent-disks.md)** and shipped in w1/m83–m86. `spec.disk {name,mountPath,sizeGB}` on the App CRD with the trade-offs enforced in CEL rather than documented — an eligible type, a paid tier, ≤1 instance, no autoscaling, grow-only sizing, and Render's mount-path denylist — projected by the operator to a per-App RWO PVC, a `Recreate` Deployment, and an autoscaler eviction guard. REST `GET/POST /v1/disks` + `GET/PATCH/DELETE /v1/disks/{diskId}` + `GET …/snapshots` + `POST …/snapshots/restore` in Render's shapes with `dsk-` ids, mirrored on GraphQL (`disks`/`disk`/`diskSnapshots`, `addDisk`/`updateDisk`/`deleteDisk`/`restoreDiskSnapshot`) and MCP (7 tools, all bex extensions — Render's MCP has none). Dashboard ships Render's **Disk** tab from a live capture of its page ([render-artifacts/disks.md](render-artifacts/disks.md)): empty-state card, five-bullet warning list, mount-path + 1/5/10/50/100 GB chips defaulting to 10, grow control that cannot express a shrink, snapshot list with restore, and Render's database-recovery warning. Blueprint `disk` is a translated handler (create default 10 GB, omission preserves, shrink fails pre-write). **Deliberate divergences:** the size form offers 10/50/100 GB where Render offers 1/5/10/50/100 — Hetzner's minimum Cloud Volume is 10 GB, so the two smaller options provisioned a 10 GB disk while billing for less, costing bex more than it charged (corrected 2026-08-24, `.pm/w1/078.md`); billed at **$0.175/GB-month** (30% off Render's $0.25, same provisioned basis — the one SKU whose estimate equals its invoice); snapshots are **file-level** (`tar`→`gzip`→`age` to object storage, 7-day retention) because Hetzner has no volume snapshots at any level, so a restore is not crash-consistent for a live database — Render's own warning applies to both; a grow may finish on the next restart (t