+#### Safe persistence transitions (w4/m153)
+
+**Runtime contract.** A mode update is accepted desired configuration, not proof that a new journal contains the current keyspace. Previously, Snapshot only → Journal + Snapshot simply restarted with AOF enabled. Valkey preferred an older journal over the newer RDB, rolling back even a dataset preserved by an explicit `SAVE` and a successful ordinary restart. The operator owns conversion for both application writers (`KeyValuePatch.apply` and `ApplyBlueprintKeyValueSpec`) and direct CR changes; REST, GraphQL, MCP, CLI, dashboard and Blueprints do not each perform their own migration.
+
+The operator projects a private startup conversion wrapper: new stores start with it; an existing store adopts it on its first actual mode change. After the old pod shuts down, the replacement privately loads the authoritative stored dataset without exposing a client listener. Journal enablement completes a successful AOF rewrite, including checks for scheduled/in-progress work, then shuts the private engine down with a saved dataset before atomically recording the applied mode. Only the completed handoff may start the desired client-facing server. Failure preserves the authoritative stored source but leaves the store pending or unavailable; it does not promise continued service from the old process. Suspended changes remain desired intent until resume and do not prematurely stamp an applied mode. Off transitions discard known historical persistence files so a later start cannot resurrect a prior dataset.
+
+The engine-level sequence follows [Valkey's AOF conversion procedure](https://valkey.io/docs/topics/persistence/#how-i-can-switch-to-aof-if-im-currently-using-dumprdb-snapshots), executed privately during startup rather than against a public running workload. The [throwaway restore runbook](../scripts/restore-keyvalue.sh) already uses RDB-first loading, AOF conversion and a verified restart. It is a sequence precedent, not the implementation used by ordinary managed-store updates.
+
+**Read and copy contract.** `persistenceMode` keeps its Render-compatible meaning as the saved desired setting (underscore wire output, underscore or hyphen input). `status` distinguishes pending application (`config_restart` for a store that has served), failure (`unavailable`) and completed current-generation readiness (`available`). Preview/dry-run requests validate without modifying the resource or starting a handoff. Existing tenant checks and protected-environment confirmation still apply. The persistence editor currently shows a protected-environment refusal without offering the server-issued retry dialog; that pre-existing UI limitation remains, while confirmed writes are supported through the API. The dashboard and MCP copy distinguish safe durable-to-durable conversion from a destructive transition involving Off. The dashboard requires its displayed `sudo change durability of key value <name>` phrase before an Off transition; repeated same-mode saves do not introduce a destructive operation. Resource identity, credentials and endpoints do not change with the mode.
+
+**Render comparison, rechecked 2026-10-02.** Render's [persistence documentation](https://render.com/docs/key-value#data-persistence) and [June 11 changelog](https://render.com/changelog/specify-disk-persistence-behavior-for-paid-key-value-instances) describe three modes, a restart on mode changes, and complete data loss when moving to or from Off. Its [update API](https://api-docs.render.com/reference/update-key-value) uses `journal_snapshot`, while [Blueprints](https://render.com/docs/blueprint-spec#key-value-specific-fields) use `journal-snapshot` and preserve omitted settings on existing paid instances. bex retains its intentional durable Free default; Render offers persistence only on paid instances. Render's authenticated edit/confirmation UI was not exercised for this correction, so the bex phrase gate is an explicit local safeguard, not a claim about Render's dialog. Post-release production keyspace survival, exact expiration deadlines, three-surface state and fixture cleanup remain separate acceptance gates; source and local tests do not satisfy them.
+
 ### 3. End-to-end public TLS
 
 The public wildcard must be **DNS-only (gray-cloud)** — Cloudflare's proxy is HTTP(S) only and cannot carry raw TCP `:6379`. Public Valkey clients use direct TLS (`rediss://`) end to end: cert-manager issues `<name>.<BEX_KV_DOMAIN>`, Valkey listens with that certificate on private port `6380`, and the shared proxy forwards the bounded ClientHello plus the remaining encrypted stream without terminating TLS. The existing internal URL remains plaintext, password-authenticated `redis://` on port `6379`; public TLS does not force in-cluster Apps through encryption or the meter.
